  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">In December 2020, the three trade union national centres in Angola, &quot;Uni&#xE3;o Nacional dos Trabalhadores Angolanos- Confedera&#xE7;&#xE3;o Sindical&quot; (UNTA-CS), &quot;Central Geral dos Sindicatos Independentes e Livres de Angola&quot; (CGSILA), and &quot;For&#xE7;a Sindical Angolana&#x2013;Central Sindical&quot; (FSA-CS), submitted a proposal to the Government calling for a review of the labour law. This was based on the outcome of a study conducted in August 2020 to assess the constraints and opportunities of the national labour law and its conformity to international labour standards and the national constitution of Angola. Following the proposel, the Government set up an extended tripartite committee named &quot;Grupo T&#xE9;cnico de Revis&#xE3;o da Lei Geral do Trabalho&quot; (GTRLGT) to examine the proposal and work on the revision and consolidation of the labour law.

The 13-member tripartite committee, composed of representatives from Government, employers&apos; and workers&apos; organisations and academics, started work in June 2021. Five sub technical groups of the committee are preparing proposals for legislative change in the different domains of the law. Upon request from the trade union national centres in Angola (UNTA-CS, CGSILA and FSA-CS), the ILO provided technical and financial support in 2020 for the engagement of a consultant to assess the constraints and opportunities of the new labour law promulgated in 2015. The study also assessed the conformity of the law to the national constitution and international labour standards. Meetings were held for the unions and the consultant to discuss the findings of the study which resulted in the elaboration of a trade union position paper on the labour law. In addition, the ILO organised a workshop in February 2020 in Luanda for the trade unions to assess the labour law vis-&#xE0;-vis the ILO&apos;s technical comments on the draft bill 7/15 submitted to the Government in 2013 during the consultation process of the bill. 

Other contributions from the ILO include:
- A regional workshop on international labour standards and ILO supervisory mechanisms for Portuguese-speaking African countries organised in February 2020 in Luanda
- Online trainings and webinars, including (1) webinar on trade unions and SDGs in Africa (May 2021); (2) webinar on understanding and mitigating the social, labour and economic impact of COVID-19 (Portuguese-speaking African countries, May 2020) ; (3) ensuring effective use of ILO tools and instruments on COVID-19 crisis and recovery - social dialogue in Francophone and Lusophone Africa in the context of COVID-19 (August 2020) ; (4) strengthening trade union engagement in the UN Development Cooperation Framework process (december 2020) ; and 
supporting social dialogue to rebuild societies and reinforce global governance (August 2021).</narrative>
   </description>
